  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: AppS&E 1
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "After a new cancer treatment is approved, doctors and patients continue
    to report side effects so the treatment\u2019s long-term safety can be monitored.\n\n<b>The
    Food and Drug Administration (FDA) oversees post-market safety by collecting
    and reviewing reports of side effects after a new treatment is approved.</b>\n\nIs
    this <b>true</b> or <b>false</b>?"
  explanation: The FDA monitors approved treatments through post-market safety programs
    that collect reports from doctors, patients, and drug manufacturers. By reviewing
    this information from across the country, the FDA can identify rare or long-term
    side effects, update safety warnings if needed, and take action to help ensure
    treatments remain safe for public use.
  correctAnswer: True
  incorrectAnswer1: False
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Approval
